PART 9 — Important Matching Rules

Follow these rules strictly.

Rule 1 — Do not match based only on names

For example:

Excel:
“Document Validation Agent”

Existing:
“PolicyDocumentProcessor”

These could still be a match if their functionality overlaps.

⸻

Rule 2 — Functional overlap is sufficient

The existing implementation does not need to match the Excel agent 100%.

If 60–80% of the required functionality already exists, identify it as a partial match and explain what needs to change.

⸻

Rule 3 — Do not duplicate functionality unnecessarily

If an existing component already performs the required capability, prefer:

reuse → extend → refactor → new development

rather than automatically creating a new agent.

⸻

Rule 4 — Do not force reuse

If the existing component is fundamentally different, mark it as:

No Match / New Agent Required

and explain why.

⸻

Rule 5 — Preserve existing implementation where possible

The goal is to introduce the persona/Role AI Agent layer while maximizing reuse of the existing Process/System capabilities.

Do not rewrite working functionality just to make naming consistent with the Excel.

⸻

Rule 6 — Follow data flow

When constructing Role AI Agent flows, use:

Input → Processing → Output → Next Agent

The output of one agent should logically satisfy the input requirement of the next agent.

If it does not, identify the mismatch instead of inventing data transformations.

⸻

Rule 7 — Distinguish Process vs System agents

Do not merge Process AI Agents and System AI Agents.

Preserve the hierarchy:

Role → Process → System

⸻

Rule 8 — Clearly identify uncertainty

If the codebase or Excel does not provide enough information to confidently determine a relationship, mark it as:

Needs Validation

Do not invent assumptions.

